export const STYLES_INDEX: string = "# Zenvi styles: the brief compiler and the one index\n\nEvery whole video starts here, before the first edit:\n\n1. **Compile the brief.** Read `BRIEF.md` (this folder) now and follow it:\n   `inspect_media_bin_tool`, the creative-direction draw, 8-12 motifs with\n   a through-line, a storyboard that does not repeat itself, the reskin test\n   and the self-critique against the style's checklist, then\n   `save_brief_tool`. Only goal, frame, length, style, hook and beats are required; the rest is advice for pieces of 20 s or more. Nothing waits for it: an agent that skips it gets a minimal auto brief on its first cut. The\n   motif method is `METHOD-motif.md`; credits in `NOTICE`. A few minutes here is\n   the difference between a reel and a slideshow.\n2. **Pick ONE style** from the table below: the row whose \"use when\" matches\n   what the first five seconds must be. Read only that style's file.\n3. **Borrow up to four techniques**, each for one named moment, from the\n   style's Mix-and-match list or the techniques table (never one the style\n   says to avoid). Read only those files.\n4. **Build it.** The style file has the tool sequence; the\n   `zenvi-motion-director` skill has the rules every genre shares and the\n   frame checks; the `zenvi-sound-designer` skill does the sound pass. Both\n   read the saved brief (`get_brief_tool`).\n5. **Check against the brief** before you say done: `review_edit_tool` and\n   the render receipts report beats, techniques and the checklist against it.\n\nMCP clients without the plugin read the same files as resources: this index\nis `zenvi://styles`, the brief compiler `zenvi://styles/brief`, each file\n`zenvi://styles/<id>`. The `zenvi_brief` prompt runs step 1.\n\nDo not read every file: this table is the whole library in one place. Tools\nlisted for a technique are what `save_brief_tool` records in its `tools`, so\nreview can see the technique on the timeline.\n\n## The index\n\n<!-- styles-index:start (generated by plugins/zenvi/scripts/gen-styles-index.mjs from front matter: do not edit) -->\n\n### Styles (12)\n\n| style | pace | aspect | use when |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [3d-product-ad](zenvi://styles/3d-product-ad) | medium | 16:9 9:16 1:1 | One product that spins in and lands - a device, a bottle, an app icon; clean studio, few cuts, one landing |\n| [data-explainer](zenvi://styles/data-explainer) | medium | 16:9 9:16 | A voice explains numbers and places - a map push-in, markers on hits, a pictogram chart, a bar race and a date clock |\n| [documentary](zenvi://styles/documentary) | medium | 16:9 9:16 | A voice-over essay or true story over B-roll - small captions, dark desaturated grade, quiet bed |\n| [editorial-type](zenvi://styles/editorial-type) | fast | 9:16 1:1 4:5 | Type is the picture - a brand or design study, crosshair grids, mono labels, letter swaps, flash frames |\n| [flash-montage](zenvi://styles/flash-montage) | frantic | 9:16 16:9 | One idea told by a new image every 3-6 frames (\"the evolution of\") - shapes matched, flashes, glow, loud |\n| [grid-montage](zenvi://styles/grid-montage) | fast | 9:16 | Many clips on one beat - a split-screen band filling a tile per beat, 3 to 4 to 6 panels, then one shot |\n| [hype-reel](zenvi://styles/hype-reel) | frantic | 9:16 16:9 | Sport, cars, launches, events, trailers, \"edit to this song\" - a cut every 0.2-0.3 s, ramps, flash and glitch drop |\n| [kinetic-captions](zenvi://styles/kinetic-captions) | fast | 9:16 | A person talking to camera where the captions are the show - word by word, emphasis words huge, behind the head |\n| [match-cut-montage](zenvi://styles/match-cut-montage) | fast | 9:16 16:9 1:1 | Shots that share a shape or pose (circles, arms out) cut so the subject never jumps - ~0.3 s shots |\n| [saas-ui-promo](zenvi://styles/saas-ui-promo) | medium | 16:9 9:16 | An app, AI tool or SaaS launch - glass UI cards over soft photos, prompt bar, counters, a URL end card |\n| [showreel-intro](zenvi://styles/showreel-intro) | fast | 16:9 9:16 | A designer's or studio's intro or showreel - project grid zooming in, 3D letters, name assembling, burst |\n| [tutorial](zenvi://styles/tutorial) | medium | 9:16 | A how-to, tips list or effect breakdown - footage in cards on grid paper, numbered steps, before/after, Save this |\n\n### Techniques (0)\n\nNone yet.\n\n<!-- styles-index:end -->\n\n## Adding a style or a technique\n\nA file in `styles/` (one per category, `kind: style`) or `techniques/`\n(`kind: technique`) with the front matter below, then\n`node plugins/zenvi/scripts/gen-styles-index.mjs` regenerates the table and\nthe MCP copy (CI fails when either is stale):\n\n```yaml\n---\nid: flash-frames\nkind: technique\nsummary: When to use it first, at most 120 characters\ncategories: [sports-hype-edit]\ntools: [apply_motion_transitions_tool]\npace: fast\nmood: [intense]\naspect: [\"9:16\", \"16:9\"]\npairs_with: []\navoid_with: []\nneeds: []\n---\n```\n\n`id` is the file name; `kind` is `style` or `technique`; `tools` are exact\nZenvi tool names; `pace` is `calm`, `medium`, `fast` or `frantic`; aspects\nare quoted (YAML reads 9:16 as a number otherwise); `pairs_with` and\n`avoid_with` are technique ids; `needs` lists missing tool ids from\n`GAPS.md`, if any. The generator does not read `#` comments: keep them out.\n";

/** BRIEF.md: the brief compiler, step 0 of every edit (zenvi://styles/brief). */
export const STYLES_BRIEF: string = "# The brief compiler (step 0 of every edit)\n\nPeople write one line (\"make a reel of my trip\", \"promo for our app, upbeat\").\nA senior editor turns that line into a brief before touching the timeline;\nthe brief is what makes the output look directed instead of assembled. Do it\nsilently (one inventory call, then thinking, then one save) and save it.\n\n**Never ask the person** unless something essential is missing and cannot be\ninferred (whose product it is when there is no name anywhere, a voice-over\nscript they clearly want read). Decide everything else, and say what you\ndecided in one line when you report. **Never invent brand facts**: names,\nprices, claims, URLs, dates, people. When the piece needs one you do not\nhave, put it in `missing`, leave a neutral placeholder out of the video (no\nfake URL, no \"Brand\" logo), and say so.\n\nNothing waits for the brief: if you cut without one, the first call saves a\nminimal brief by itself (style matched from your words, the frame, the\nlength, one beat) and says so. That keeps review honest, but it plans\nnothing; a brief of your own is what makes the edit directed.\n\n## 1. Read what you have\n\n- The prompt: who it is for, where it goes, what it must make someone do.\n  Their exact words, names and colours go into the video verbatim.\n- `inspect_media_bin_tool` (one call, a few hundred tokens): every file's\n  kind, length, orientation, speech, faces, colours and energy, plus hints.\n  Do not open files one by one before this.\n- A reference link or \"like this one\": `ingest_web_video_tool` with\n  `intent: \"reference\"` and take pace and look from it, never its content.\n\n## 2. Infer the frame\n\n| Prompt says | Platform | Aspect | Length |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| reel, TikTok, short, story, nothing | Reels / TikTok / Shorts | 9:16 | 15 s (8-30) |\n| YouTube, trailer, website hero, LinkedIn video | YouTube / web | 16:9 | 20-30 s |\n| feed post, carousel video | Instagram feed | 4:5 or 1:1 | 10-15 s |\n| ad, promo with no platform | Reels first | 9:16 | 15 s |\n\nMost footage portrait (the inventory says so): 9:16 even if unsaid. A song in\nthe bin: the length lands on its phrase (8 or 16 bars at its tempo).\nA voice-over: the length is the voice plus 1-1.5 s of end card.\n\n## 3. Pick the style, the techniques and the direction\n\nOpen the index (`SKILL.md`). Pick ONE style by what the first five seconds\nmust be, not by the subject. Then at most four techniques, each borrowed for\none named moment and a reason (\"flash frames on the drop at 7.5 s: the\nenergy peak\"). More than four, or one with no moment, is a sampler, not a\nstyle. Never borrow what the style's Mix-and-match says to avoid. Copy each\ntechnique's `tools` from the index into the brief.\n\n**3b. The creative-direction draw.** Templates come from habit: the same\nlook, rhythm and opening every time. `read_director_recipe {styles: \"brief\"}`\nends with a fresh draw (art style, palette, type, rhythm, camera,\ntransitions, finish, copy tone, opening, ending) that avoids the last edit's\npicks on this machine. Take at least 3 axes into **direction**, each with a\n`why`: keep the drawn pick, or re-draw one that fights the subject and say\nwhy; never slide back to the last edit's picks. The save receipt says when\nyou did.\n\n## 4. Motifs, then the storyboard\n\nThe method is `METHOD-motif.md` (this folder; after Tweenkit's\nkinetic-cut-video, see NOTICE); in short:\n\n- **motifs**: 8-12 visual elements only THIS subject has, at least one from\n  each of: its tools and instruments, materials and textures, signs and\n  units, screens and charts, places and history, the one gesture of the\n  trade (for a coffee brand: grinder, crema, roast curve, TDS reading, the\n  farm's altitude, the pour). Pick the **through_line**: the motif that\n  recurs and opens and closes the piece.\n- **storyboard**: one row per shot. `start`, `beats` (length in beats),\n  `picture`, `motif` (which motif it grows from), `source` (`media` with its\n  `file_id`, or `stock` with the query, `generate` with the prompt,\n  `motion`, `3d`, `title`), `composition`, `text_entrance`, `tone`\n  (brightness and main colour), `transition` (a motif match cut first: the\n  waveform becomes the next shot's trace). The last four columns exist to\n  see repetition before cutting. The rules (advice, never a refusal: from\n  20 s, or with `check_method: true`, the save receipt lists what breaks them):\n  - first and last shot grow from the through-line; every motif is a listed one;\n  - lengths vary (1 / 2 / 4 / 8 beats in a curve: set-up, speed-up, breath,\n    climax, hold), never all the same;\n  - no composition more than twice (centred, object left / type right, full\n    bleed, type cropped by the frame, split, diagonal, empty space);\n  - three or more text entrances (slam, typed, built from the motif, mask\n    wipe, flip, stretch);\n  - at least one shot on a bright ground, inverted or filled with the accent;\n  - exactly one `climax` row, whose tone no other shot has: the heaviest\n    moment breaks the system the rest set up.\n  Get 1.5x the media the storyboard needs; the style gives the source count.\n\n## 5. Write the rest of the brief\n\nEvery field is one or two lines.\n\n- **goal**: what the viewer must do or feel after one watch.\n- **audience**, **platform**, **aspect**, **length_seconds**.\n- **style** and **techniques** (id, why, tools).\n- **hook**: the first 2 s exactly: what is on screen at 0.0 s and what is\n  heard. Motion and sound from frame 1; no logo, no \"Welcome to\", no fade\n  from black. The strongest image or the boldest claim goes first.\n- **beats**: the structure with timings (the style's table at this length),\n  on the music's grid when there is music: hook, build, turn, payoff / drop,\n  end card. A picture change starts every beat.\n- **type**: two families at most, sizes, how type moves (the style's type\n  system); **palette**: the look or LUT and one accent (from their brand or\n  the subject's own materials, never a default).\n- **music**: tempo, energy curve (where it builds, where it drops, the\n  stillness before the climax), from the bin or one search;\n  **sound**: what lands on cuts, UI events, the climax (the style's sound\n  palette); **captions**: who speaks and how words appear, or none.\n- **end_card**: what holds at the end (their name, their URL, a product\n  shot); only facts they gave.\n- **checklist**: the style's finish checklist, copied (at most 12 lines).\n\n## 6. Critique it, then fix it\n\nBefore saving, read the brief against the style's \"what separates the best\nfrom average\" (its Finish checklist; for a director recipe its Checks and\nPitfalls) and against these, and rewrite what fails:\n\n- **The reskin test.** For each storyboard row: if the copy named another\n  subject (a coffee brand instead of a university), would this picture still\n  work? Count those rows in **reskin.generic**. More than a third is a\n  template: regrow those shots from the motifs and count again. The save receipt flags over a third.\n- Does the hook work with the sound off, and does it hit in the first 0.5 s?\n- Is there one bold moment, and only one? Is there stillness before it?\n- Does every beat start with a picture change and end before it drags (the\n  style's longest shot)? Is anything held 2 s in a fast style?\n- Is every technique tied to a moment? Would a viewer notice it missing?\n- Is there enough media for the cut rate (no clip back within 3 s)?\n- Does the end card hold 1 s or more on a fact they gave?\n- Is anything invented: a name, a claim, a number, a URL? Move it to `missing`.\n\nWrite what you changed in **critique** (one line per fix). Then:\n\n```json\nsave_brief_tool {\"goal\": \"Make runners want the new trail shoe after one watch\", \"platform\": \"Instagram Reels\", \"aspect\": \"9:16\", \"length_seconds\": 15, \"style\": \"hype-reel\", \"techniques\": [{\"id\": \"cinematic-speed-ramp\", \"why\": \"the wind-up into the drop at 7.5 s\", \"tools\": [\"speed_ramp_tool\"]}], \"hook\": \"Mud splashes off a sole in slow motion, impact on frame 1, sub hit\", \"beats\": [{\"start\": 0, \"end\": 2, \"name\": \"hook\"}, {\"start\": 2, \"end\": 7.5, \"name\": \"build\"}, {\"start\": 7.5, \"end\": 13.5, \"name\": \"drop\"}, {\"start\": 13.5, \"end\": 15, \"name\": \"end card\"}], \"motifs\": [\"lugged sole\", \"mud spray\", \"laces pulled tight\", \"trail map contour lines\", \"altitude readout\", \"GPS pace screen\", \"rock and root texture\", \"the stride\"], \"through_line\": \"lugged sole\", \"storyboard\": [{\"start\": 0, \"beats\": 4, \"picture\": \"lugged sole hits wet mud in slow motion\", \"motif\": \"lugged sole\", \"source\": \"stock\", \"composition\": \"full bleed\", \"text_entrance\": \"none\", \"tone\": \"dark wet earth\"}, {\"start\": 2, \"beats\": 2, \"picture\": \"contour lines draw on over the ridge\", \"motif\": \"trail map contour lines\", \"source\": \"motion\", \"composition\": \"object left / type right\", \"text_entrance\": \"typed\", \"tone\": \"bright paper map\", \"transition\": \"contour line becomes the trail\"}, {\"start\": 3, \"beats\": 1, \"picture\": \"laces yanked tight, macro\", \"motif\": \"laces pulled tight\", \"source\": \"stock\", \"composition\": \"diagonal\", \"text_entrance\": \"slam\", \"tone\": \"dark, orange accent\"}, {\"start\": 3.5, \"beats\": 8, \"picture\": \"altitude counts up as the runner crests\", \"motif\": \"altitude readout\", \"source\": \"motion\", \"composition\": \"type cropped by the frame\", \"text_entrance\": \"built from the motif\", \"tone\": \"orange accent fill\", \"climax\": true}, {\"start\": 7.5, \"beats\": 4, \"picture\": \"the sole again, now clean, held as a poster\", \"motif\": \"lugged sole\", \"source\": \"stock\", \"composition\": \"centred\", \"text_entrance\": \"mask wipe\", \"tone\": \"dark, orange accent\"}], \"reskin\": {\"generic\": 1, \"note\": \"the laces macro could sell any shoe\"}, \"direction\": [{\"axis\": \"art style\", \"pick\": \"blueprint line drawing\", \"why\": \"trail maps are line drawings; last edit was film documentary\"}, {\"axis\": \"rhythm\", \"pick\": \"slow start, fast finish\", \"why\": \"the climb, then the descent\"}, {\"axis\": \"opening\", \"pick\": \"straight into the hit\", \"why\": \"the sole strike is the hook\"}], \"music\": \"128 BPM, quiet build, drop at 7.5 s\", \"checklist\": [\"a hit on every cut\", \"one look on every shot\", \"ends on a held product shot\"], \"critique\": \"Moved the logo off the hook; the drop had no stillness before it, added 0.4 s hold; replaced a generic sunset with the altitude readout\"}\n```\n\nThe receipt checks the brief against the project at once (frame, length).\nThen set the project to the brief's frame (`set_project_setting_tool`) and\nbuild it with the style's tool sequence.\n\n## 7. Use it while you edit\n\n- The brief is the plan: the director's plan table is the brief's storyboard\n  with frames and a sound per row.\n- `get_brief_tool` before each stage of a long edit (cut, type, look, sound)\n  and when unsure what comes next. It reports beats without a picture change\n  and borrowed techniques the timeline does not show yet.\n- The edit beats the brief: when a better idea appears, `save_brief_tool`\n  again with it (one undo step) rather than drifting silently.\n- Before done: `review_edit_tool` lists the brief's misses and its checklist;\n  look for every checklist line on the contact sheet. Render receipts and\n  `review_render_tool` repeat the hard misses (\"Off its brief\"): fix them or\n  change the brief on purpose.\n";

